Z||000000||nette||04-14-2001||11:16 PM||||Hello...another new Fiero owner here, long-time Pontiac enthusiast though (66 Grand Prix).<P>I have an unidentifiable noise in the right front.  Tone of sound changes according to your speed.  It's kind of a low-pitched growling/howling sound.  Inspected outer bearing and it seemed fine.  Husband said he inspected inner bearing when he did the brakes and they looked fine(?).<P>It's an 87 coupe, 5sp 4cyl.  Any ideas?<P>------------------<BR>nette<BR>87 coupe||||reg||5
Z||000001||V8Fiero||04-14-2001||11:19 PM||||Had the same sound on my car. Changed the bearings and that was it. I would change them anyway. If your husband had the car apart to look at them shouldnt be a problem changing them....besides its only like $10 a side to fix...<P>I suspect this is your noise.<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||04-14-2001||11:21 PM||||If it were me, I'd check them again and make sure my brake pads were not dragging. Did it make this noise before the brake job, or did it start after?<BR>Might check the dust shield behind the rotor and insure there is no contact (with the rotor) or rock or something in it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phaeton||04-15-2001||01:48 PM||||"growl" was the exact word the shop guy used to describe why he replaced my front hub.  It spun fine, no drag I could detect.  But I trust the guy and he said replace it as soon as it starts to talk.||||reg||12
Z||000004||MrPBody||04-15-2001||03:13 PM||||You say the sound comes from right front.  Is it louder when turning left?  If so, then I think it's almost certainly wheel bearings.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||04-15-2001||05:04 PM||||As old as they likely are... replacing wheel bearings is cheap insurance. the bearing will often make noise before it looks bad. It can fail very sudenly once it starts making noise.<P>If you have a bearing driver it doesn't take long either. You might be able to borrow one from a parts store. they aren't very expencive. The basic one sold under the Lisle brand fits most cars and light trucks. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||nette||04-15-2001||10:18 PM||||Thanks so much for all the great responses.  You are all confirming my thoughts and since bearings are inexpensive I am running down to the parts store tomorrow.<P>The noise tends to get louder when turning the wheel to the right most of the time, but under some conditions it's louder when turning the wheel to the left.<P>I am also hearing a resonating noise behind me at highway speeds, no matter what road I am on.  I was thinking this might be the rear bearing assembly hub but I want to be more sure of this diagnosis before I shell out $160 for a pair.  How hard are these to replace?  Or is it just normal tranny noise?<P>------------------<BR>nette<BR>87 coupe||||reg||7
